/*!
    \example r6botteachpendant
    \title R6 Robot Teach Pendant
    \ingroup qtros2-examples
    \meta sortkey {80}
    \brief A teach pendant for a 6-DOF arm: a live 3D model driven by TF, joint
    jogging and waypoint programming driven by joint trajectories.

    \c{R6 Robot Teach Pendant} is a teach pendant for the 6-DOF \c r6bot arm from
    \l {https://github.com/ros-controls/ros2_control_demos/tree/master/example_7}{ros2_control example 7}.
    It shows the arm as a live Qt Quick 3D model driven by TF, and lets the
    operator jog individual joints, capture named waypoints, and replay them as
    a looping sequence — commanding the arm through
    \c trajectory_msgs/JointTrajectory messages. It is the most involved example
    of driving a robot (as opposed to monitoring one), and a thorough
    demonstration of driving a 3D model from TF with
    \l {QtRos2.Quick3D::FrameNode}{FrameNode}.

    \image r6botteachpendant/r6botteachpendant.jpg "The R6 Robot Teach Pendant: a TF-driven 3D model of the arm with joint-axis triads, and the Jog panel with per-joint controls and a speed profile."

    \section1 Running the example

    \list 1
        \li Build the \c ros2_control demos in a workspace, in a ROS-sourced
            terminal:
            \badcode
            mkdir -p ~/ros2_ws/src && cd ~/ros2_ws/src
            git clone https://github.com/ros-controls/ros2_control_demos -b ${ROS_DISTRO}
            cd ~/ros2_ws/
            rosdep install --from-paths ./ -i -y --rosdistro ${ROS_DISTRO}
            colcon build --merge-install
            \endcode
        \li Source your ROS 2 environment and launch Qt Creator from that shell.
        \li Open \c examples/r6botteachpendant/CMakeLists.txt in Qt Creator, then
            build and run.
        \li Launch the r6bot simulation in a separate terminal:
            \badcode
            source ~/ros2_ws/install/setup.bash
            ros2 launch ros2_control_demo_example_7 r6bot_controller.launch.py
            \endcode
    \endlist

    \section1 Forwarding command-line arguments to ROS

    Because a teach pendant may be launched with ROS arguments (for remapping or
    parameters), \c main.cpp initializes the ROS 2 context explicitly, forwarding
    \c argc/\c argv, before constructing the Qt application:

    \code
    #include <QtRos2Core/qros2context.h>

    int main(int argc, char *argv[])
    {
        QRos2Context::init(argc, argv);      // hand ROS args to rclcpp
        QGuiApplication app(argc, argv);
        QQmlApplicationEngine engine;
        engine.loadFromModule("R6BotTeachPendant", "Main");
        return app.exec();
    }
    \endcode

    \section1 The view model

    All ROS logic is centralized in \c R6BotViewModel.qml — no other QML file
    imports the bridge — which keeps the UI files purely presentational. It
    declares one \c Node hosting three entities:

    \qml
    import QtRos2.Core
    import QtRos2.SensorMsgs as SensorMsgs
    import QtRos2.TrajectoryMsgs as TrajectoryMsgs

    Node {
        id: ros2Node
        nodeName: "qt_r6bot_teach_pendant"

        SensorMsgs.JointStateSubscriber { id: jointStateSubscriber; topic: "/joint_states" }
        TrajectoryMsgs.JointTrajectoryPublisher {
            id: trajectoryPublisher
            topic: "/r6bot_controller/joint_trajectory"
        }
    }
    \endqml

    The CMake side enables the publisher/subscriber capabilities and links the
    trajectory message module. TF is consumed through \c QtRos2.Quick3D
    (\c FrameNode), which uses \c tf2_ros in C++, so no \c tf2_msgs QML
    module import is required:

    \badcode
    qt_ros2_configure_target(r6botteachpendant
        CAPABILITIES PUBLISHER SUBSCRIBER
        MODULES QtRos2TrajectoryMessages
    )
    \endcode

    \section1 Reading joint state

    The \c JointStateSubscriber's message drives the numeric panels. The view
    model derives a few convenient properties from it, including a \c moving flag
    used to sequence replay:

    \qml
    readonly property list<real> currentPositions: jointStateSubscriber.message.position
    readonly property bool moving:
        jointStateSubscriber.message.velocity.some(vel => vel !== 0.0)
    \endqml

    \section1 Commanding the arm

    Motion commands are \c trajectory_msgs/JointTrajectory messages. The view
    model echoes the joint names from \c /joint_states, then builds a list of
    interpolated points ending in a zero-velocity settling point, and publishes:

    \qml
    trajectoryPublisher.publish({
        header: { stamp: { sec: 0, nanosec: 0 }, frameId: "" },
        jointNames: jointStateSubscriber.message.name,   // echo names from /joint_states
        points: points                                   // {positions, velocities, timeFromStart}
    })
    \endqml

    Jogging a single joint applies a delta to one position and sends a short
    single-point trajectory; the jog step sizes (Fine/Normal/Fast) are 0.02, 0.1,
    and 0.2 radians.

    \section1 Driving the 3D model from TF

    The 3D robot is driven entirely by TF, not by joint angles directly — the
    \c robot_state_publisher in the r6bot stack turns \c /joint_states into \c /tf,
    and the pendant consumes that. \c R6BotModel.qml is a chain of
    \l {QtRos2.Quick3D::FrameNode}{FrameNode} items mirroring the URDF: each one names its
    link's frame, and follows it relative to the enclosing FrameNode. Only the
    root needs to know which \l Node to read the TF tree from:

    \qml
    FrameNode {
        id: worldNode
        frame: "world"

        FrameNode {
            id: baseLinkNode
            frame: "base_link"

            FrameNode {
                id: link1Node
                frame: "link_1"
                // Joint: revolute, axis: [0.0, 0.0, 1.0]
                position: Qt.vector3d(0.0, 0.0, 0.061584)

                Link_1 { ... }      // the link's mesh
                ...
            }
        }
    }
    \endqml

    Two details are worth copying into your own code:

    \list
        \li The positions and rotations written in \c R6BotModel.qml are the
            URDF rest pose. A FrameNode keeps what it was given until its
            transform arrives, so they show a sensible pose before then.
        \li Plain \c Node items between FrameNodes are for presentation only:
            here, each link's mesh sits in one, offset by the URDF \c <visual>
            origin. The whole model is also scaled from meters to millimeters
            and rotated to map ROS Z-up into Qt Quick 3D's Y-up convention,
            outside the root FrameNode.
    \endlist

    Each FrameNode's \c valid property tells whether TF has placed it. The
    model's \c ready property combines them, and gates its visibility.

    \section1 Waypoints and replay

    Waypoints capture the \e joint positions (from \c /joint_states), not TF, and
    are held in memory for the session. Replay is velocity-driven rather than
    timed: after sending a waypoint, the view model waits for the arm to come to
    rest (all joint velocities return to zero) before sending the next one, using
    the \c moving transition:

    \qml
    onMovingChanged: {
        if (wasMoving && !moving) {           // the arm just stopped
            if (nextWaypointToSend >= waypoints.length) {
                if (loopEnabled) { restartFromFirstWaypoint() }
                else            { executing = false }
            } else {
                sendNextWaypoint()
            }
        }
        wasMoving = moving
    }
    \endqml

    Per-waypoint duration is derived from the largest joint delta, and
    \e stopping publishes the current position with zeroed velocities to halt
    smoothly.

    \section1 The UI at a glance

    \list
        \li A status bar shows the live joint readout from \c /joint_states.
        \li The large 3D view shows the arm (with an optional per-joint axis
            triad) driven by TF.
        \li A tabbed side panel offers \e Jog (per-joint +/- with step size),
            \e Waypoints (capture, list, jump-to), and \e Replay (play sequence,
            progress, loop).
    \endlist

    \sa {Robot Monitor}, {Robot Arm Collision}, {Qt ROS2 Quick3D QML Types}
*/
